## Environment Setup

*Instructions on how to set up the isolated computational environment to run the analysis scripts. Choose the package manager that fits your workflow.*

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/username/{{project_name}}.git
cd {{project_name}}
```

### Option 1: uv

```bash
# uv sync automatically creates the .venv and installs dependencies from pyproject.toml
uv sync
source .venv/bin/activate  # On Windows use `.venv\Scripts\activate`, or run via WSL2 terminal
```

### Option 2: Standard pip (venv)

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate  # On Windows use `.venv\Scripts\activate`, or run via WSL2 terminal
pip install .              # Installs dependencies listed in pyproject.toml
```

### Option 3: Poetry

```bash
poetry install
poetry shell
```

## Usage

*Explain how to run the project. Provide examples of the commands needed to run data pipelines, training scripts, or start up applications/dashboards.*

{% if include_module -%}
The package lives under `src/{{module_name}}` (src layout) — once you've run `uv sync`, it installs in editable mode, so it's importable from anywhere in the project without extra path setup:

```bash
# Example: Run the data ingestion pipeline
uv run python -m {{module_name}}.data.make_dataset

# Example: Train the model
uv run python -m {{module_name}}.models.train_model
```
{% else -%}
This project has no installable package — it's a sandbox for notebooks and standalone scripts. Organize scripts under `src/` (`data/`, `features/`, `models/`, `visualization/`) and save any trained models to `models/` at the project root.

```bash
# Example: run a standalone script
uv run python some_script.py
```
{% endif %}

(`make_dataset.py` and `train_model.py` are placeholders — add scripts under `src/{{module_name}}/data` and `src/{{module_name}}/models` as your pipeline takes shape.)

